    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The translation of expanded DNA repeats lacking an AUG start codon has been implicated in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), a progressive and universally fatal neurodegenerative disease. While the RNA transcripts and proteins produced by repetitive regions of the C9ORF72 gene have been characterized as toxic and correlate with disease, how these repeats are able to circumvent the conventional mechanisms of translation remain unknown. The field has begun to uncover genes responsible for driving this form of translation, but a model for the non-canonical mechanism remains unresolved, as is an understanding of how it contributes to ALS and other repeat-associated diseases. We have developed a GFP reporter with which we are able to visualize these abnormal translation products, called dipeptide repeat (DPR) proteins, using fluorescence microscopy. Using shRNA knockdown, we have investigated the role of hnRNP A1 in this possible disease pathway of ALS. We also saw a possible but equivocal reduction in DPR protein product when hnRNP A1 was knocked down in HEK293T cells with shRNAs. Given the prominence of repeat expansions in ALS and other neurodegenerative diseases—Huntington Disease, several spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA), and Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome (FXTAS)—understanding how these sequences are translated and finding the genes responsible can reveal therapeutic targets for treating these diseases.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
